The material trade-off is that serializable simplifies reasoning but increases aborts under contention. Decide using measured request volume, tail latency, contention, recovery objectives, and the consequence of stale or duplicated work. Write those assumptions into an ADR and expose the chosen guarantee to callers; otherwise different teams will infer incompatible semantics. In a real deployment I would start with one bounded path, shadow or canary it, compare old and new outcomes, and retain a rollback switch.

Operationally, instrument attempts, latency, saturation, rejected work, and terminal outcomes. Use low-cardinality reason codes, propagate context, and make dashboards distinguish caller cancellation from dependency failure. Set explicit queue, connection, and concurrency limits. A load test should include slow dependencies, partial network loss, process restarts, duplicate delivery, and cancellation—not just the happy-path throughput number.

Before launch, add contract tests around the invariant, race-enabled tests where state is shared, and an integration test using the real dependency. Define an SLO and alert on multi-window burn rather than isolated errors. Roll out by tenant or traffic percentage, watch both correctness and resource use, then remove the compatibility path only after its observed use reaches zero.

