### Why this `reduce`-based grouping works:
- Each `key` like `color__eq` becomes a projection into `result[color][eq]`
- Default-assigning `acc[root] ?? {}` ensures no undefined access errors
- No intermediate state mutation beyond the accumulator (a functional norm per lodash/ramda style)

If you *must* preserve all values (e.g., `?color__in=red&color__in=blue`), extend it:

```ts
const grouped = Object.entries(params)
  .reduce<Record<string, Record<string, string | string[]>>>((acc, [key, value]) => {
    if (typeof value !== 'string' || !key.includes('__')) return acc;
    const [root, field] = key.split('__');
    if (!root || !field) return acc;

    acc[root] = {
      ...(acc[root] ?? {}),
      [field]: (acc[root]?.[field] as string[] | undefined)
        ? [...(acc[root][field] as string[]), value]
        : value,
    };
    return acc;
  }, {});
```

This avoids the original bug (silent overwrites) and stays composable—e.g., chaining `Object.entries(grouped).filter(...)` for schema validation.

